feat(dashboard): draw the mark in the shell, and tab the run detail (RM-140)

Two things the design produced and the build never showed.

THE MARK. web/public/innsegl-mark.svg shipped in every build and nothing under
web/src referenced it, so the only place the seal ever appeared was a 16px
browser tab. The shell's header now draws it beside the wordmark. It is drawn
inline rather than fetched, for the reason Icon.tsx gives the icon set, and it
is aria-hidden with focusable="false", so the header still says "Innsegl"
exactly once to a screen reader. The asset stays the source of truth: FE-112
reads public/innsegl-mark.svg and compares it element for element and attribute
for attribute against what the header paints, so the two cannot drift, and it
asserts the header took the reading-size weight rather than the favicon's
heavier one.

That test found a second defect on the way in. Both public/innsegl-mark.svg and
public/favicon.svg carried a CSS custom property name inside an XML comment. An
XML comment may not contain a double hyphen, SVG is XML, and xmllint refuses
both files outright, which means favicon.svg had almost certainly never
rendered in a tab at all. The comments are reworded, the drawings are
untouched, and FE-112 parses both assets and fails if either stops being a
well-formed document.

THE RUN DETAIL. The activity log sat below the timeline, and the ordering was
right: doc 06 P3 puts the strongest claim first and the timeline is the
evidence chain. The reach was wrong. Measured on a real run of 1220 tool calls,
"below" meant several hundred screens below, and the activity log was
unreachable in practice. The two are now one tab control with the timeline
first and selected, so the ordering argument survives intact while second
costs one key instead of one scroll to the bottom of the page.

Three properties are held by FE-113. The integrity banner renders outside both
panels and stays on screen whichever tab is open, because a condition hidden
behind an unselected tab is worse than the scroll this replaced. Each tab
carries its own count, the timeline's events and the activity log's tool calls,
so a reader knows what is behind the tab they are not looking at. And the
control is a real tablist with aria-selected, aria-controls, a roving tabindex
and Left, Right, Home and End, because the tab role is a promise about which
keys work.

Timeline.tsx and ActivityLog.tsx are unchanged. This is composition.

